afk: an explicit task.isolation.mode pins the isolation backend — no
silent fallback.

Motivation: upstream treats the configured backend as a hint.
ensureIsolation (task/worktree.ts) walks isoResolve's whole candidate
list, so when the pinned backend's start fails (e.g. overlayfs: kernel
mount EPERM inside a sandbox without a payload-owned userns) the spawn
silently downgrades — typically to rcopy. rcopy materialises snapshots
via `git worktree add` + dirty-state seeding, which drops all gitignored
files from the snapshot (subagent task briefs under .superpowers/sdd/
vanished), and the downgrade masks the actual environment breakage. afk
ships task.isolation.mode: overlayfs in the distro config for sandboxes
whose payload owns a mount-capable user namespace, so overlayfs is a hard
requirement: a spawn that cannot get it must fail loudly, not copy.

Scope: ensureIsolation only. `auto` (preferred === undefined) keeps the
full resolver fallback chain and the fellBack/fallbackReason reporting;
an explicit backend restricts the candidate list to exactly that
backend, so an unavailable start surfaces as the thrown fallbackReason
(the probe/mount denial message). Internal backend flexibility is
untouched (the Overlayfs backend still tries fuse-overlayfs itself).

What must survive a rebase:
- ensureIsolation: candidates = [preferred] when preferred is set; the
  resolver chain is consulted only for auto mode.
- Tests (test/task/worktree.test.ts): a pinned backend whose isoStart
  fails ISO_UNAVAILABLE rejects instead of trying other candidates; a
  pinned backend that starts never consults the fallback chain.
- Error surfacing: a pinned failure reports the pinned backend's own
  isoStart error; resolution.reason (which may describe an unrelated
  auto-chain candidate, e.g. "btrfs CLI not on PATH") is used only in
  auto mode.

